  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "The residual has an unexamined ranking property: without clipping, log(p(j|i)/q_j) = log(1+c_ij/(alpha*q_j)) − log(1+n_i/alpha). With candidate-independent anchor weights, the second term cancels in candidate rankings. Row support therefore does not independently attenuate ranking changes; clipping can change this behavior. Include an explicit support-weighting ablation.",
    "Cross-fitting is underspecified. The diagnostic loader supplies no user IDs, so recent item IDs alone cannot reliably identify the user's excluded fold. Specify fold propagation without changing the public loader contract, and ensure each training-position residual uses only its causal sequence prefix.",
    "The report lacks exact folds, hyperparameter ranges, prior smoothing, clipping thresholds, recency weights, score calibration, and trial allocation. It also needs paired validation uncertainty and protection against repeatedly tuning on the same 800 users.",
    "Originality 5/10 and difficulty 3/10 are provisional: established sequential nearest-neighbor and Markov baselines are missing, while fold-aware diagnostics, sparse full-catalog scoring, and predictor parity add implementation work. No validation evidence yet supports preferring this residual over the objective-focused alternative."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Check whether popularity normalization and clipping actually provide the intended sparse-evidence protection, and identify appropriate simple baselines.",
      "query": "In sequential recommendation, how do Dirichlet-smoothed transition log-lift scores compare with first-order Markov chains, sequential rules, and V-SKNN? For p(j|i)=(c_ij+alpha*q_j)/(n_i+alpha), the row-normalization term cancels from candidate rankings before clipping: what support weighting or Bayesian evidence measures prevent rare low-popularity edges from dominating?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Resolve reproducibility and leakage risks before implementing fold-dependent scoring.",
      "query": "How should user-grouped out-of-fold features be implemented for sequential recommendation stacking when diagnostic batches contain only (None, sequence, positive, negative), with no user IDs? Seek designs that preserve user-to-fold provenance, causal prefix alignment, and matching inference semantics, and address distribution shifts when rebuilding transition tables from all training users."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Define a credible, budgeted comparison without modifying the pinned evaluator or overstating sampled-ranking gains.",
      "query": "What validation practices control selection optimism when tuning recommender score fusion on a fixed benchmark of four datasets, 200 validation users each, and 99 sampled negatives per user? Seek paired user-bootstrap uncertainty for macro HR@10, prespecified small ablations against unchanged SASRec and multi-negative objectives, and efficient reuse of frozen model scores."
    }
  ]
}
